<header><p class="st-kicker">caliphate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Massina Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1818 CE – 1862 CE · capital Hamdullahi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Fulbe Islamic state in the inner Niger delta, ruled from Hamdullahi, which tried to build a model Muslim society under a council of scholars.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1818 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulbe</a> preacher in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a> region of the inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta, led a revolt against the Fulbe ardo chiefs and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bambara</a> overlords of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bamana-empire">Bamana Empire</a>, winning a battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-noukouma">Noukouma</a>. He proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, the Diina, and around 1820 founded a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a>, the House of Praise.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> ruled with a Great Council of forty scholars, imposed strict Islamic law, banned tobacco and dancing, and built schools. The state regulated the seasonal movements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulbe</a> herds through the delta, settling pastoral life into a code that endured long after. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a> took Djenne and, from 1826, controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadu-seku">Amadu Seku</a> and grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadu-amadu">Amadu Amadu</a> succeeded him.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-hadj-umar-tall">Umar Tall</a>, a Tijani rather than a Qadiri, led to war. In 1862 Umar took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a> and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadu-amadu">Amadu Amadu</a> executed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/toucouleur-empire">Toucouleur Empire</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulbe</a> resistance killed Umar in 1864.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/massina-empire.jpg" alt="Massina Empire" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Massina_Empire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1818 CE – 1862 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>caliphate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hamdullahi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Seku Amadu, Amadu Seku, Amadu Amadu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Qadiriyya)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Fulfulde</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Caliphate of Hamdullahi, Diina of Massina, Macina Empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=massina-empire" aria-label="Massina Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4568 1644 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Massina Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4888 1900L4830 1933M4888 1900L4918 1811"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4830 1933h0M4918 1811h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4888 1900h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4888 1900h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hamdullahi. <a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=massina-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
